.\" Manpage for cchef.
.TH CCHEF 1 "2026-08-02" "cchef" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cchef \- CyberChef on the command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cchef
.I operation
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ input ]
.br
.B cchef bake
.BR \-e | \-r
.I recipe
.RI [ input ]
.br
.B cchef
.RB [ list | url | recipe | version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cchef
is a command-line port of the data-transformation engine of CyberChef, the
"Cyber Swiss Army Knife". Every CyberChef operation is a subcommand that reads
input and writes output, so operations chain through Unix pipes or run together
as a single recipe, and any recipe can be turned into a shareable CyberChef URL.
.PP
Each operation reproduces its CyberChef counterpart's output. The operations
this build is aligned with match CyberChef 11.3.0; run
.B cchef \-\-version
to confirm.
.PP
This tool is not recommended for production or critical-infrastructure use.
.SH INPUT AND OUTPUT
An operation takes its input, in order of precedence, from a positional
argument, the
.B \-i
/
.B \-\-input
flag, the
.B \-\-in\-file
flag, or standard input. Output goes to standard output, or to a file with
.B \-o
/
.BR \-\-output .
Output is byte\-exact when piped or redirected; a trailing newline is added only
when writing to a terminal. Use
.B \-\-in\-file \-
or
.B \-\-output \-
to force standard input or output within a pipeline.
.PP
Point an operation (or
.BR bake )
at a directory with
.B \-\-in\-dir
to run it once per file, top\-level by default or
.B \-\-recursive
to walk subdirectories; results go to standard output with
.RI \(dq==> name <==\(dq
headers, or to
.B \-\-out\-dir
as one output file per input.
.SH GLOBAL FLAGS
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-input " " \fIstring\fR
Input data as a literal string.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIstring\fR
Write output to a file (default standard output).
.TP
.B \-\-in\-file \fIpath\fR
Read input from a file.
.TP
.B \-\-in\-dir \fIdir\fR
Run the operation over every file in a directory.
.TP
.B \-\-out\-dir \fIdir\fR
Write output into a directory, one file per input.
.TP
.B \-\-recursive
With
.BR \-\-in\-dir ,
recurse into subdirectories.
.TP
.B \-\-preview
Display image output inline (iTerm2, WezTerm or kitty terminals).
.TP
.B \-\-data\-uri
Write output as a
.RI \(dq data:<mime>;base64,... \(dq
URI.
.TP
.B \-\-ansi \fIauto|always|never\fR
Use ANSI color when writing to a terminal (default
.BR auto ).
Applies to highlighted output and to the
.B recipe show
listing.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Help for a command.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and the aligned CyberChef release.
.SH READING VALUES FROM FILES
Every string\-valued argument flag has a
.BI \-\- flag \-file
companion that reads the value from a file instead of the command line, keeping
keys and passphrases out of shell history and process listings. One trailing
newline is stripped. Giving both a flag and its
.B \-file
companion is an error.
.SH RECIPES
A recipe is an ordered list of operations in one of two auto\-detected formats:
JSON,
.RI \(dq [{\(dqop\(dq:\(dqTo Base64\(dq}] \(dq,
or the compact Chef format,
.RI \(dq To_Base64('A\-Za\-z0\-9+/=') \(dq.
Run one with
.B cchef bake \-e
.I recipe
or
.B \-r
.IR file
.RB ( \-r " " \-
reads it from standard input), convert between the formats with
.BR "cchef recipe convert" ,
or turn it into a CyberChef share URL with
.BR "cchef url" .
.PP
.B \-\-from\-url
reads the recipe back out of a CyberChef share URL, so a link can be run
directly. Nothing is fetched: the recipe travels in the link. A link that
carries an input uses it when the command line names none. Only one of
.BR \-e ", " \-r " and " \-\-from\-url
may be given.
.PP
A recipe can also be staged interactively with
.B cchef recipe add
and edited with
.BR show ", " rm ", " move ", " toggle " and " clear .
.B cchef recipe load
replaces the whole staged recipe with one read from
.BR \-e ", " \-r " or " \-\-from\-url ,
and
.B cchef recipe convert
prints the staged recipe back out in either format.
The staged recipe lives in
.B .cchef\-recipe.json
in the working directory;
.BR bake ", " url " and " "recipe convert"
use it when given no recipe of their own.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
List every operation, grouped by category, with a one\-line summary.
.TP
.B bake
Run a whole recipe against the input.
.TP
.B url
Print a CyberChef share URL for a recipe and optional input.
.TP
.B recipe
Stage and edit a recipe step by step;
.B recipe load
replaces the staged recipe and
.B recipe convert
converts between JSON and Chef formats.
.B recipe show
marks each step
.RB \(dq [X] \(dq
when it runs or
.RB \(dq [\ ] \(dq
when it is disabled.
.TP
.B version
Print version information.
.PP
Run
.B cchef list
for the full set of operation subcommands, and
.B cchef
.I operation
.B \-\-help
for one operation's flags.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CCHEF_BASE_URL
Base URL for
.BR "cchef url" ,
overriding the public CyberChef instance.
.TP
.B CCHEF_RECIPE
Path to the staged\-recipe file, overriding
.B .cchef\-recipe.json
in the working directory.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set, disables ANSI color output (see also
.BR \-\-ansi ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cchef/config.yaml
Optional configuration file (defaults to
.B ~/.config/cchef/config.yaml
).
.TP
.B .cchef\-recipe.json
The interactively staged recipe, in the working directory.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B cchef
exits 0 on success and non\-zero if an operation errors or an input file cannot
be processed. Unlike CyberChef, which renders an operation error as its output,
cchef writes the error to standard error and exits non\-zero.
.SH OPTIONAL DEPENDENCIES
The
.B Optical Character Recognition
operation drives an installed
.BR tesseract (1)
binary and returns a clear error if it is not present. Every other operation is
self\-contained: cchef is a single static binary with no other runtime
dependency.
.SH SEE ALSO
Full per\-operation documentation is at
https://github.com/roberson-io/cchef .
.SH BUGS
Report issues at
https://github.com/roberson-io/cchef/issues .
Because cchef's behavior is defined by CyberChef's, a divergence between the two
is the clearest possible report; include the recipe or a
.I gchq.github.io/CyberChef
URL demonstrating the expected output.
.SH AUTHOR
Michael Roberson. cchef is an independent port of CyberChef (GCHQ, Crown
Copyright) and is not affiliated with or endorsed by GCHQ.
